Imagine you're designing a car. Before you build actual cars, you need a blueprint that defines:
- What features it has (color, model, engine type)
- What it can do (drive, brake, honk)

A class is exactly that - a blueprint for creating objects!

• **Properties (Fields)**: These are the characteristics (like color, model)
• **Methods**: These are the actions (like drive(), brake())

Here's the simplest class structure:
```java
class Car {
    // Fields (properties)
    String color;
    String model;

    // Methods (behaviors)
    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Vroom vroom!");
    }
}
```

• The `class` keyword starts the definition
• Curly braces `{}` contain all the class members
• Fields store data, methods define behavior

